  ],
  "text": "One bad night raises the hormone that makes you hungry and lowers the one that says stop. You treat sleep and eating as two separate problems. Your body does not. Ghrelin is the one that tells you to eat. After a short night it goes up. Leptin is the one that says you have had enough. After a short night it goes down. Both controls pushed the wrong way, before you have made a single decision. And the craving is not random. It skews hard towards high calorie food. A tired brain wants fast energy, and it wants it now. Insulin sensitivity drops too, after only a few short nights. Which means the same meal does more damage on less sleep. Sleep is not the opposite of doing something. It is one of the levers. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
